1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,320
Hello, English learners.

2
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:05,760
Welcome back to EnglishPod.

3
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:06,200
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:07,560
And my name is Catherine.

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,600
And today, I think we're hitting the gym, Marco.

6
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:11,840
We need to be healthy.

7
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:13,980
We've gained weight over the winter, I think.

8
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:14,700
I think so.

9
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:22,520
And being in an office, you know, it doesn't give you much opportunity to go and exercise and really get a lot of work done during the day.

10
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:23,520
Exactly.

11
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:25,520
So that's why we're going to the gym on this occasion.

12
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:27,080
We're going to be pumping some iron.

13
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:28,800
We're going to be working out.

14
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:31,900
So why don't we take a look at Vocabulary Preview and see what this means.

15
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:34,180
Vocabulary Preview.

16
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:35,800
All right.

17
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:37,250
So, to pump iron, what does that mean?

18
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:38,640
All right.

19
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:40,770
Well, to pumping iron means lifting weights.

20
00:00:41,230 --> 00:00:41,390
Okay.

21
00:00:41,650 --> 00:00:42,950
So we're pumping iron.

22
00:00:43,150 --> 00:00:44,280
We're lifting weights.

23
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:45,960
Because, you know, weights are made of iron.

24
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:46,960
Right.

25
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:47,960
That's what makes them so heavy.

26
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:48,960
All right.

27
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:49,960
So we're pumping iron.

28
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:50,960
Good.

29
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:52,850
And, well, we're going to be giving you guys some pointers.

30
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:53,990
Pointers.

31
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:54,840
Right.

32
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:56,200
All right.

33
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:57,200
So you might have heard the word point before.

34
00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:58,920
And pointers is another word for tips.

35
00:00:58,920 --> 00:00:59,920
All right.

36
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:01,150
So we're going to be giving you tips or advice.

37
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:01,670
Yeah.

38
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:04,010
So you're going to get some help today from the trainer.

39
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:05,240
All right.

40
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:06,840
So we're going to jump right into this dialogue.

41
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:16,280
And when we come back, we'll be talking more about what's going on.

42
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:16,530
Hey there.

43
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:17,910
You look a little lost.

44
00:01:18,390 --> 00:01:19,110
Are you new here?

45
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:20,160
Yeah.

46
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:21,100
How did you know?

47
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:23,260
You can always spot the newbies.

48
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:25,480
I can give you a few pointers if you want.

49
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:27,380
Were you trying to use this machine here?

50
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:28,120
Yeah.

51
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:31,400
I just started my training today, and I'm not sure where to begin.

52
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:32,900
It's okay.

53
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:34,400
I know how it is.

54
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:38,780
This machine here will work out your upper body, mainly your triceps and biceps.

55
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:42,380
Are you looking to develop strength or muscle tone and definition?

56
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:45,320
Well, I don't want to be ripped like you.

57
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:48,020
I just want a good physique with weights and cardio.

58
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:50,700
In that case, you want to work with less weight.

59
00:01:51,340 --> 00:01:54,200
You can start off by working 10 to 15 reps in four sets.

60
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:56,240
Five kilo weights should be enough.

61
00:01:57,080 --> 00:01:59,160
Now it's very important that you stretch before pumping iron.

62
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:00,140
You might pull a muscle.

63
00:02:00,820 --> 00:02:01,320
Got it.

64
00:02:01,870 --> 00:02:02,310
Wow.

65
00:02:02,310 --> 00:02:03,870
Is that the weight you're lifting?

66
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:06,800
My goodness, that's a lot of weight.

67
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:07,410
It's not that much.

68
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:08,250
Just watch.

69
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:16,360
I'm okay.

70
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:22,490
So this guy was a little bit arrogant.

71
00:02:22,970 --> 00:02:28,330
I think he was very muscular, but I think he embarrassed himself in front of the lady.

72
00:02:28,550 --> 00:02:29,370
The trainer, you mean?

73
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:30,640
Yeah.

74
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:31,640
Yeah, definitely.

75
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:32,970
So we say he had a big ego.

76
00:02:33,310 --> 00:02:34,770
He thought a lot about himself.

77
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:44,130
So let's check out some of these phrases and words that they were using because they're all related to going to the gym, and they can help you if you ever want to get some help when you're at the gym.

78
00:02:50,570 --> 00:02:52,330
So this girl, she was new in the gym.

79
00:02:52,530 --> 00:02:53,890
She didn't really know what she was doing.

80
00:02:54,170 --> 00:02:58,650
And well, the guy said, well, do you want to develop strength or muscle tone?

81
00:02:59,130 --> 00:02:59,510
All right.

82
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:02,160
We know what strength is, but what is muscle tone?

83
00:03:02,410 --> 00:03:02,790
Okay.

84
00:03:03,030 --> 00:03:11,410
So if you want to develop muscle tone or to have muscle tone, it means that you have healthy muscles, not like huge, big muscles, right?

85
00:03:11,570 --> 00:03:13,350
But you have a nice body.

86
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:14,920
Right.

87
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:19,640
So tone, to tone is to maybe give some shape to something.

88
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:23,580
Sometimes you have big arms and you can't see the muscles.

89
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:27,880
Well, muscle tone means that you can start to see the shape and they start to get stronger.

90
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:28,880
All right.

91
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:32,460
So if you think of Madonna, maybe five years ago, she had a lot of muscle tone.

92
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:35,560
Now she's like big muscles.

93
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:38,140
But muscle tone means that you can see the muscles, they start to develop.

94
00:03:38,660 --> 00:03:38,920
Okay.

95
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:44,480
And this actually is very related to our next word because you said muscle tone and definition.

96
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:45,480
Right.

97
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:57,140
So definition is another way for us to describe seeing those muscles because at first you have a big tummy or you have big arms and when you start to work out, every time you go, you can see more and more definition.

98
00:03:57,620 --> 00:04:00,080
That means more and more of the muscle becomes visible.

99
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:01,080
All right.

100
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:02,320
So you have defined muscles.

101
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:03,120
All right.

102
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:04,560
Good.

103
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:06,960
Now the girl was like, no, no, I don't want to be like super strong and huge.

104
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:09,080
She doesn't want to look like Arnold Schwarzenegger.

105
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:09,560
Yeah, exactly.

106
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:11,400
She said, I just want a good physique.

107
00:04:12,150 --> 00:04:12,470
All right.

108
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:18,120
So this word maybe sounds strange for some of you because it's more like French than English.

109
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:19,120
Yeah.

110
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:22,000
Well, it does come from French and physique is a way to talk about the whole body.

111
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,140
You know, the physique is the state of your whole body.

112
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:26,990
It's your physical appearance.

113
00:04:27,430 --> 00:04:27,590
Okay.

114
00:04:27,610 --> 00:04:30,330
So I can say she has a nice physique.

115
00:04:31,310 --> 00:04:34,370
So that means she's probably works out a lot.

116
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:38,000
So but basically it's like her whole body, right?

117
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,200
You can't just say like she has good physique in her arms or something like that.

118
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:45,080
And now your physique, physical, think of the word physical.

119
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:46,830
It's your whole body, your whole body.

120
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:47,270
All right.

121
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:48,430
So good physique.

122
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:56,480
And now the tips that he was giving her or the pointers, he was talking about these two words reps and sets.

123
00:04:56,480 --> 00:04:57,480
Right.

124
00:04:57,480 --> 00:05:01,910
These are great words for the gym because they, they allow us to count when we're working out.

125
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:05,040
So the first one is reps or repetitions.

126
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:07,940
That means doing something over and over again.

127
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:16,560
So for example, if I want to lift a weight, I sit down on the bench, I do one, two, three.

128
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:20,280
How many times I lift that weight is a repetition or a rep.

129
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:20,440
All right.

130
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:23,840
And well, and what about sets?

131
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:28,680
So I could say I lift the weight 10 times, one, two, three, four, all the way to 10.

132
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:31,220
And then I wait and then I do it again.

133
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:34,760
So the number of times I do it again is a set.

134
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:37,720
So sets of 10 or sets of five.

135
00:05:37,720 --> 00:05:38,720
Okay.

136
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:49,410
So if you are lifting this weight 10 times and you have five sets, you will do a total of 50 pushes or lifts or repetitions.

137
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:49,730
Okay.

138
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:57,040
So reps and sets are probably the most important or common words you'll hear in the gym, especially if you're lifting weights.

139
00:05:57,040 --> 00:05:58,040
Right.

140
00:05:58,040 --> 00:05:59,040
Definitely.

141
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:03,130
And sometimes your trainer will count with you so that he'll, he'll keep a count while you're working out.

142
00:06:04,190 --> 00:06:04,390
Cool.

143
00:06:04,710 --> 00:06:07,650
So those are the words that we have for you on Language Takeaway.

144
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:20,280
I think in order to better understand these words, we should listen to our dialogue again, but we're going to slow it down just a little bit.

145
00:06:20,280 --> 00:06:20,610
Hey there.

146
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:22,170
You look a little lost.

147
00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:23,590
Are you new here?

148
00:06:24,110 --> 00:06:24,510
Yeah.

149
00:06:24,750 --> 00:06:25,350
How did you know?

150
00:06:25,870 --> 00:06:28,330
You can always spot the newbies.

151
00:06:28,890 --> 00:06:31,270
I can give you a few pointers if you want.

152
00:06:32,030 --> 00:06:33,870
Were you trying to use this machine here?

153
00:06:34,430 --> 00:06:34,990
Yeah.

154
00:06:35,230 --> 00:06:37,030
I just started my training today.

155
00:06:37,370 --> 00:06:38,910
I'm not really sure where to begin.

156
00:06:39,530 --> 00:06:40,050
It's okay.

157
00:06:40,630 --> 00:06:41,690
I know how it is.

158
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:47,060
This machine here will work out your upper body, mainly your triceps and biceps.

159
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:51,320
Are you looking to develop strength or muscle tone and definition?

160
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,640
Well, I don't want to be ripped like you.

161
00:06:55,140 --> 00:06:57,980
I just want a good physique with weights and cardio.

162
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:01,320
In that case, you want to work with less weight.

163
00:07:02,120 --> 00:07:05,460
You can start off by working 10 to 15 reps in four sets.

164
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:07,660
Five kilo weights should be enough.

165
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:13,100
Now, it's very important that you stretch before pumping iron or you might pull a muscle.

166
00:07:13,780 --> 00:07:14,260
Got it.

167
00:07:14,830 --> 00:07:15,210
Wow.

168
00:07:15,530 --> 00:07:16,670
Is that the weight you're lifting?

169
00:07:17,350 --> 00:07:18,930
My goodness, that's a lot of weight.

170
00:07:19,610 --> 00:07:20,650
It's not that much.

171
00:07:21,310 --> 00:07:21,790
Just watch.

172
00:07:26,390 --> 00:07:27,170
I'm okay.

173
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:28,680
All right.

174
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:36,260
So, you might have heard some other phrases in there that sound interesting or new to you.

175
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:41,720
That's why we're going to go into the fluency builder part of the lesson where we can talk about sounding more fluent in English.

176
00:07:43,510 --> 00:07:44,250
Fluency builder.

177
00:07:45,210 --> 00:07:45,710
All right.

178
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:48,310
So, the girl, she was a little bit lost, right?

179
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:50,240
So, this guy was trying to help her out.

180
00:07:50,250 --> 00:07:51,690
I think it was her first time in the gym.

181
00:07:52,490 --> 00:07:52,830
It was.

182
00:07:53,310 --> 00:07:58,590
She doesn't really know where to start, which is a common problem for people who go to the gym for the first time.

183
00:07:58,730 --> 00:08:03,170
So, the guy said, well, yeah, I know how it is.

184
00:08:03,650 --> 00:08:04,230
How what is?

185
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:05,800
Exactly.

186
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:06,800
How what is?

187
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:09,120
It's a very strange phrase because it's vague.

188
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:09,640
It's not clear.

189
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:12,060
So, what is he referring to?

190
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:16,240
This is something very common that we say in English to mean, I understand your situation.

191
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:17,080
I know how you feel.

192
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:18,180
I know how you feel.

193
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:20,480
So, she says, this is my first time.

194
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:22,060
I don't know what to do.

195
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:24,860
And he says, I know how it is.

196
00:08:25,120 --> 00:08:26,240
I understand your situation.

197
00:08:26,320 --> 00:08:30,600
Right, because I imagine the first time he went to the gym, he felt the same way.

198
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:31,600
Exactly.

199
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:33,810
Or I could say, I'm so lost.

200
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:34,710
I'm new at this school.

201
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:35,890
I don't even know anyone.

202
00:08:36,290 --> 00:08:37,250
Yeah, I know how it is.

203
00:08:37,250 --> 00:08:38,630
I was new here last year.

204
00:08:38,750 --> 00:08:40,550
So, trust me, I know how it is.

205
00:08:40,760 --> 00:08:41,760
Okay.

206
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:45,400
So, Marco and I have similar situations, although he has already experienced it.

207
00:08:45,510 --> 00:08:46,350
So, he can help me out.

208
00:08:46,670 --> 00:08:47,490
This is a good phrase.

209
00:08:47,770 --> 00:08:52,130
It's actually, I think it's very common for somebody to kind of sympathize with you.

210
00:08:52,910 --> 00:08:53,270
Absolutely.

211
00:08:53,570 --> 00:08:56,790
And it's a really good phrase if you want to help someone who looks lost or confused.

212
00:08:57,290 --> 00:09:04,530
So, if someone joins your work, if you have a new colleague, and they say, oh, I don't know where anything is.

213
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:07,120
I don't know how to do anything at my job.

214
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:08,390
You say, oh, I know how it is.

215
00:09:08,510 --> 00:09:10,030
I was new here last month.

216
00:09:10,610 --> 00:09:10,970
Great.

217
00:09:11,610 --> 00:09:15,510
And, well, now they were talking about, you know, lifting weights and the physique.

218
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:17,410
And she said this interesting word.

219
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:20,030
She says, I don't want to be ripped like you.

220
00:09:20,570 --> 00:09:20,930
Okay.

221
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:25,760
And many of you have probably heard the other meaning of the word to rip or ripped.

222
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:26,550
So, this is to rip.

223
00:09:27,150 --> 00:09:27,910
Yeah, to tear.

224
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:29,810
But in this case, it's an adjective.

225
00:09:30,200 --> 00:09:32,990
It's a way to describe someone's physique, their body.

226
00:09:33,720 --> 00:09:37,010
And we're talking about people who are very, very muscular.

227
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:38,360
Okay.

228
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:40,050
So, basically, bodybuilders.

229
00:09:40,840 --> 00:09:42,250
Arnold Schwarzenegger is ripped.

230
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:44,090
He has huge muscles.

231
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:46,050
Madonna is ripped.

232
00:09:46,460 --> 00:09:47,490
She has huge muscles.

233
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:48,800
Okay.

234
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:52,850
So, it's a very informal way, but it's a very informal way of saying very muscular.

235
00:09:53,330 --> 00:09:53,570
Yeah.

236
00:09:53,650 --> 00:09:55,540
It's someone who works out a lot.

237
00:09:56,380 --> 00:09:56,720
All right.

238
00:09:56,860 --> 00:09:57,280
You're ripped.

239
00:09:57,800 --> 00:09:59,180
And after this, we've got one more phrase.

240
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:05,480
It's actually very important because when we talk about going to the gym, working out, we have to talk about safety and your health as well.

241
00:10:05,480 --> 00:10:06,780
It's very easy to hurt yourself.

242
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:07,220
Right.

243
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:09,540
And that's what we want to avoid.

244
00:10:10,040 --> 00:10:12,000
You don't want to pull a muscle.

245
00:10:12,660 --> 00:10:12,800
All right.

246
00:10:12,940 --> 00:10:13,760
Pull a muscle.

247
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:19,280
And this is a key phrase here because we have a special combination of verb and noun.

248
00:10:19,280 --> 00:10:20,740
The thing that we pull is our muscle.

249
00:10:21,520 --> 00:10:23,560
And what we do to it is we pull it.

250
00:10:23,560 --> 00:10:24,920
That means we hurt it.

251
00:10:25,160 --> 00:10:30,840
So if you don't stretch and you're lifting weights, maybe your muscle will start to hurt and you can't lift anymore.

252
00:10:31,160 --> 00:10:31,300
Yeah.

253
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:32,140
You hurt it.

254
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:33,020
You pulled your muscle.

255
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:35,240
You pulled a muscle.

256
00:10:35,240 --> 00:10:36,240
Or often runners have this problem.

257
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:38,020
They don't stretch before they run or they run too much.

258
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:40,340
And they have a lot of pain in their muscles.

259
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:41,700
You say, I pulled a muscle.

